menu "OpenReader"

    config OPENREADER_OSDP_ADDRESS
        int "OSDP PD address"
        range 0 126
        default 0
        help
            The 7-bit address this PD answers (0x00..0x7E). Must match what
            the ACU polls. The broadcast address 0x7F is always answered in
            addition and is not configurable.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_BAUD
        int "RS-485 baud rate"
        default 9600
        help
            Must match the ACU. OSDP defines 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600,
            115200 and 230400. 9600 is the spec's mandatory default and the
            right choice for first bring-up.

    config OPENREADER_DISPLAY
        bool "Show the reader face on the onboard LCD"
        default y
        help
            Draws a virtual reader on the 1.47 inch ST7789: a colour disc
            mirroring the WS2812 (the ACU's osdp_LED colour while the link
            is up, the blue breath while it is down), the PD address, the
            link state, and a card panel showing the last UID read.

            The panel is the only thing that must have hardware SPI2 — its
            clock and data are fixed on-board at GPIO7 and GPIO6 and cannot
            be moved. An RC522 on bit-banged SPI runs alongside it happily.

    config OPENREADER_RC522
        bool "External MFRC522 card reader"
        default y
        help
            Reads ISO/IEC 14443-A cards and reports each UID to the ACU as
            osdp_RAW. Turn off for a display-only or bus-only build.

    choice OPENREADER_RC522_BUS
        prompt "How the RC522 is clocked"
        depends on OPENREADER_RC522
        default OPENREADER_RC522_SOFT_SPI
        help
            The ESP32-C6 has one general-purpose SPI master and an SPI host
            has one set of bus pins. When the LCD is built it owns that
            host, because the panel's pins are fixed and the RC522's are
            ours to choose — so the RC522 is the one that gives way.

            Giving way costs very little. The hardware path clocks at
            5 MHz and the software path at roughly 500 kHz, but a card poll
            is only a few dozen register accesses; at ten polls a second
            the difference is well under a millisecond of CPU per second,
            against an OSDP tick that must not be late by more than a few.

    config OPENREADER_RC522_HW_SPI
        bool "Hardware SPI2 — requires the LCD to be off"
        depends on !OPENREADER_DISPLAY
        help
            SPI2 in hardware, on the RC522's header pins. Only selectable
            with the display disabled, since the two cannot share the host.

    config OPENREADER_RC522_SOFT_SPI
        bool "Bit-banged SPI — runs alongside the LCD"
        help
            Clocks the RC522 in software on the same header pins, leaving
            hardware SPI2 free for the panel. This is what lets the reader
            face and real card reads coexist.

    endchoice

    config OPENREADER_BUZZER
        bool "Audible output — active sounder on GPIO18"
        default n
        help
            Drives an active buzzer from osdp_BUZ. "Active" means the part
            makes its own tone, so the pin is simply on or off; the library
            resolves the command's on/off/count pattern and hands the reader
            the edges.

            Default off, and deliberately so: enabling this also makes
            osdp_CAP report an audible output, and claiming a sounder that
            is not fitted is a lie the ACU may act on — it will command
            beeps that never happen and have no way to find out. Turn it on
            when the part is actually on the header.

    config OPENREADER_BUZZER_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "The sounder sounds when the pin is LOW"
        depends on OPENREADER_BUZZER
        default n
        help
            Cheap three-pin modules disagree about polarity. If yours
            shrieks from boot and goes quiet only when the ACU commands a
            beep, it is active low — set this.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_TX_GPIO
        int "RS-485 UART TX GPIO (to the transceiver's RXD)"
        default 0
        help
            Which pin drives the SH-U12's RXD input.

            The default (GPIO0, header GP0) is a free header GPIO rather
            than the pins silkscreened TX/RX. GPIO18/19 are not usable: 19
            is the RC522's MISO (board.h) and the build rejects the clash. Those labelled pins are
            GPIO16/17 — UART0's default mapping — and they work fine here,
            with one caveat: the ROM bootloader transmits its banner on
            GPIO16 at every reset, so on a live multi-drop bus that chatter
            lands on the wire before your firmware runs.

            Waveshare has shipped more than one revision of this carrier and
            their published documentation does not enumerate the header, so
            trust your board's silkscreen over any default here.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_RX_GPIO
        int "RS-485 UART RX GPIO (from the transceiver's TXD, via the divider)"
        default 1
        help
            Which pin reads the SH-U12's TXD output. Default GPIO1 (header
            GP1).

            Remember this pin must never see the transceiver's raw 5 V — it
            reaches the ESP32-C6 through the resistor divider. See
            docs/HARDWARE.md.

    config OPENREADER_RS485_ECHO_SUPPRESS
        bool "Discard local echo of transmitted bytes"
        default n
        help
            The MAX13487 on the DSD TECH SH-U12 handles direction
            automatically, with no DE/RE pin to toggle. Some auto-direction
            transceivers leave the receiver enabled while driving, so every
            byte the PD transmits comes straight back in on RX. The OSDP
            stream decoder would then have to chew through the PD's own
            replies looking for a frame.

            Enable this and the transport drops exactly as many inbound
            bytes as it just transmitted.

            LEAVE THIS OFF until you have measured your board — see
            docs/HARDWARE.md, "Does your transceiver echo?". If the board
            does not echo and this is on, it will eat the first bytes of the
            ACU's next command and the PD will never answer a poll.

    config OPENREADER_CARD_REPEAT_MS
        int "Ignore the same card for this many milliseconds"
        default 2000
        help
            A card left on the antenna is re-read several times a second.
            Reporting each read would flood the ACU with duplicate osdp_RAW
            replies, so an identical UID inside this window is suppressed.

    config OPENREADER_PDID_SERIAL
        hex "PD serial number reported in osdp_PDID"
        default 0x00000001
        help
            Reported in the osdp_PDID reply. Give each unit on a bus a
            distinct value; some ACUs use it to tell devices apart.

endmenu
